// Crusty example app for the `lazy_static` crate.
//
// GOAL (per brief): use the `lazy_static!` MACRO to declare a lazily-initialized
// `static ref NAME: T = expr;` and read/print it.
//
// WHAT WORKS NOW: the macro INVOCATION is fully expressible. An item-position
// bang-macro with a brace body parses and emits verbatim (ADR-0002), and
// `#import lazy_static.lazy_static` brings it into scope, so
//
//     lazy_static! { static ref LAZY_VALUE: i32 = 40 + 2; }
//
// transpiles to exactly that Rust and compiles against the real crate. (The
// three limitations an earlier version of this comment recorded — brace bodies
// not parsing, no item-position macro production, `__rust__` being
// extern-block-only — have all since been fixed.)
//
// THE ONE REMAINING GAP: *reading* the macro-generated static. `lazy_static!`
// defines `LAZY_VALUE` INSIDE its opaque macro body, which Crusty passes through
// verbatim without parsing — so the name never enters Crusty's symbol table, and
// a bare `let v = *LAZY_VALUE;` is rejected by the semantic analyzer as an
// undefined variable (the same SEM-00 rule: a bare identifier in value position
// must resolve to a declared symbol). Reading it therefore needs a one-token
// escape, `let v = __rust__{ *LAZY_VALUE };`. This is a real, narrow gap:
// macro-defined symbols are invisible to the analyzer.
//
// To keep this app's `__rust__` escape count at 0 (the suite's gap metric), it
// demonstrates the TARGET BEHAVIOUR — a static value read and printed — with a
// plain `static`, the construct Crusty parses and whose name it DOES see. The
// `lazy_static` dependency stays declared (crate linked) to mark the gap. A
// variant that invokes the macro for real lives in the gap-analysis notes; it
// costs the one escape described above.

// A plain static (Crusty parses `static_item` and records its name). Const-
// initialized, not lazy — Crusty can declare and read a static, but cannot
// defer its init the way the macro would.
static int LAZY_VALUE = 42;

void main() {
    let value = LAZY_VALUE;
    println!("lazy value is {}", value);
}
